Hi all,

A common tool in economics is the structural VAR, which uses hard constraints on elements of the VAR coefficient and covariance matrices to help identify an economic model (I'm using "identify" in the economics sense here, as in, loosely, "pin down the causal relationships"). For instance, take a very simple toy model with Y(t) = (USGDP_growth(t), Australan_GDP_growth(t), Australian_unemployment(t))' and specification

Y(t) ~ multi_normal(A + BY(t-1),  Sigma)

where A and B are coefficient matrices (no contemporaneous effects). From an economic perspective, it won't make sense to let B be freely estimated. We'd expect the US economy to exert an effect on the Australian economy (1/15th the size), but not visa versa. Yet US and Australian economic performance will be correlated and an unrestricted estimate of B will say that Australian economic output affects US economic output when we run shocks through the model.

Ideally, I'd want to do something like

parameters{
matrix[3, 3] B;
B[1,2] <- 0;
B[1,3] <- 0;
}

Instead I'm doing something like

transformed parameters {
  matrix[3,3] B1;
  for(i in 1:3){
    for(j in 1:3){
    if(i==1 && j==2){
      B1[i,j] <- 0;
    }
    if(i==1 && j ==3){
      B1[i,j] <- 0;
    }
    else {
      B1[i,j] <- B[i,j];
    }
  }}
}

In which case I'm declaring parameters (in B) that don't get used in the model. Is this going to be a problem?

Another option would be to provide very tight priors over the relevant elements of B. I don't personally object to this, though it's not standard.

(Note I've not implemented one of these models in Stan yet, but I am considering a large project and I'm trying to think through the potential issues).

Cheers,
Jim

